1
00:00:01,020 --> 00:00:03,579
Two patterns are already on the wafer.

2
00:00:03,579 --> 00:00:07,082
Alignment marks came first,
then the active islands.

3
00:00:07,082 --> 00:00:09,776
After that the gate stack was deposited.

4
00:00:09,776 --> 00:00:11,729
Our task is the gate stripes.

5
00:00:11,729 --> 00:00:14,760
The next page shows what
we are cutting into.

6
00:00:16,787 --> 00:00:19,577
This is the stack we are cutting into.

7
00:00:19,577 --> 00:00:24,790
The panel under the photo is a
cross-section through one single island.

8
00:00:24,790 --> 00:00:28,975
The thicknesses differ by up
to five orders of magnitude.

9
00:00:28,975 --> 00:00:34,482
So the drawing lies about thickness on
purpose, to keep all layers visible.

10
00:00:34,482 --> 00:00:37,786
The top layer is the
one we are going to cut.

11
00:00:37,786 --> 00:00:41,458
A hundred nanometres of
n-doped amorphous silicon.

12
00:00:41,458 --> 00:00:45,863
Under it, there's seven and a
half nanometres of gate oxide:

13
00:00:45,863 --> 00:00:48,139
the thinnest film on the wafer.

14
00:00:48,139 --> 00:00:52,251
The gate etching has to stop
on it, and leave it intact.

15
00:00:52,251 --> 00:00:55,555
The three below are the
ones we must not cut.

16
00:00:55,555 --> 00:00:59,153
Thirty nanometres of
silicon - the active island,

17
00:00:59,153 --> 00:01:03,045
the film the transistor
channel is actually built in.

18
00:01:03,045 --> 00:01:04,660
145 nanometres of BOX.

19
00:01:04,660 --> 00:01:07,818
And then 725 micrometres of handle silicon.

20
00:01:07,818 --> 00:01:13,104
Before any etching, one more film will
be deposited on top of the stack.

21
00:01:13,104 --> 00:01:15,087
That film is the hard mask.

22
00:01:17,120 --> 00:01:23,269
One more film goes on top of the stack:
forty nanometres of silicon dioxide,

23
00:01:23,269 --> 00:01:28,366
put down by PECVD (plasma-enhanced
chemical vapour deposition).

24
00:01:28,366 --> 00:01:33,949
Silane and nitrous oxide go in; the
plasma breaks them into radicals,

25
00:01:33,949 --> 00:01:37,833
and the radicals build
the oxide on the surface.

26
00:01:37,833 --> 00:01:42,040
The layer growing on the
cross-section is that film.

27
00:01:42,040 --> 00:01:45,924
Forty nanometres is chosen
for what comes later:

28
00:01:45,924 --> 00:01:50,940
this is the hard mask, and it
has to survive the silicon etch.

29
00:01:52,987 --> 00:01:56,173
The thickness is set by the deposition time,

30
00:01:56,173 --> 00:02:02,256
so it has to be measured - and it's hard
for an ellipsometry to do it on a patterned

31
00:02:02,256 --> 00:02:03,356
wafer.

32
00:02:03,356 --> 00:02:05,673
So a second wafer, bare silicon,

33
00:02:05,673 --> 00:02:09,728
goes through the same process
and gets measured instead.

34
00:02:09,728 --> 00:02:15,787
And that wafer will be measured twice today:
once now, and once after the main etch.

35
00:02:17,653 --> 00:02:21,165
Ellipsometry does not
measure thickness directly.

36
00:02:21,165 --> 00:02:26,252
It sends polarised light in and reads
the polarisation that comes back:

37
00:02:26,252 --> 00:02:29,477
linear before the wafer,
elliptical after it.

38
00:02:29,477 --> 00:02:34,063
The two angles are the measurement
- psi is the amplitude ratio,

39
00:02:34,063 --> 00:02:35,998
delta the phase difference.

40
00:02:35,998 --> 00:02:41,086
Fit them across a range of wavelengths
and two things come out at once:

41
00:02:41,086 --> 00:02:43,307
refractive index and thickness.

42
00:02:43,307 --> 00:02:45,959
Here we get film is 37.02 nanometres.

43
00:02:45,959 --> 00:02:50,975
We measure five points: the centre,
and four at sixty millimetres out.

44
00:02:50,975 --> 00:02:54,414
The five agree to plus or
minus 0.12 nanometres.

45
00:02:54,414 --> 00:02:56,636
That means the film is uniform.

46
00:02:56,636 --> 00:03:00,864
Against the forty-nanometre
target it is 7.4 per cent thin,

47
00:03:00,864 --> 00:03:03,157
inside the ten-nanometre window.

48
00:03:03,157 --> 00:03:04,073
It passes.

49
00:03:05,920 --> 00:03:10,269
The oxide we just deposited is
hydrophilic: it ends in OH groups,

50
00:03:10,269 --> 00:03:13,615
it holds water, and resist
does not stick to that.

51
00:03:13,615 --> 00:03:19,369
So there is one more step before the resist
- dry the wafer, then make it repel water.

52
00:03:19,369 --> 00:03:22,045
We pump down and bake: the water leaves.

53
00:03:22,045 --> 00:03:25,391
HMDS vapour goes in and
reacts with the OH groups.

54
00:03:25,391 --> 00:03:31,212
What is left is a monolayer - one end bonded
to the oxide, the methyl end pointing out.

55
00:03:31,212 --> 00:03:32,617
Then purge, and vent.

56
00:03:32,617 --> 00:03:33,888
One molecule thick.

57
00:03:33,888 --> 00:03:37,501
It changes no geometry; it
changes the surface energy.

58
00:03:37,501 --> 00:03:40,780
The oxide now repels water
instead of holding it.

59
00:03:42,820 --> 00:03:47,923
Resist, 1.2 micrometres - and it
goes onto a wafer that is spinning:

60
00:03:47,923 --> 00:03:49,499
5000 rpm, 30 seconds.

61
00:03:49,499 --> 00:03:52,726
The film is still liquid while it spins up,

62
00:03:52,726 --> 00:03:57,979
and that is when the thickness is
decided - faster spin, thinner film.

63
00:03:57,979 --> 00:04:00,906
The spin also throws resist to the rim,

64
00:04:00,906 --> 00:04:05,858
where it piles up into a bead
several times thicker than the film.

65
00:04:05,858 --> 00:04:11,862
That bead dries unevenly, flakes, and
dirties everything the wafer touches next.

66
00:04:11,862 --> 00:04:17,040
So it is rinsed off from the wafer:
the rim first, then the backside.

67
00:04:19,087 --> 00:04:22,580
The plate comes up under
the wafer at 95 degrees.

68
00:04:22,580 --> 00:04:25,573
The film left the spinner full of solvent.

69
00:04:25,573 --> 00:04:27,284
The solvent leaves here.

70
00:04:27,284 --> 00:04:31,989
95 degrees sits below the glass
transition so the film won't flow.

71
00:04:31,989 --> 00:04:34,911
The solvent leaves, and the film hardens.

72
00:04:34,911 --> 00:04:36,123
It does not melt.

73
00:04:36,123 --> 00:04:40,543
Two things are settled here: One
is the adhesion to the oxide.

74
00:04:40,543 --> 00:04:44,107
The other is the stress
the spin left in the film.

75
00:04:46,153 --> 00:04:49,289
A stepper does not expose
the whole wafer at once.

76
00:04:49,289 --> 00:04:52,988
It projects the reticle onto
one field, steps, and repeats.

77
00:04:52,988 --> 00:04:58,005
Field by field means the lens only has to
be good over a few square centimetres,

78
00:04:58,005 --> 00:05:01,642
and the reticle only has to
carry one copy of the pattern.

79
00:05:01,642 --> 00:05:03,649
The light does not draw a shape.

80
00:05:03,649 --> 00:05:06,470
It changes how fast the
resist will dissolve.

81
00:05:06,470 --> 00:05:08,853
The shape only appears at development.

82
00:05:10,887 --> 00:05:13,711
The light does not stop at the surface.

83
00:05:13,711 --> 00:05:19,721
It goes through the resist, reflects off
the silicon underneath, and comes back up.

84
00:05:19,721 --> 00:05:24,790
Incident and reflected interfere, so
the intensity ripples with depth.

85
00:05:24,790 --> 00:05:28,410
So the film is not exposed
uniformly along height.

86
00:05:28,410 --> 00:05:32,610
At 110 degrees the photoproduct
diffuses from high to low.

87
00:05:32,610 --> 00:05:35,507
That flattens the concentration profile.

88
00:05:37,553 --> 00:05:39,020
TMAH, 2.6 per cent.

89
00:05:39,020 --> 00:05:42,264
Thirty seconds, then thirty seconds again.

90
00:05:42,264 --> 00:05:45,507
Where the resist was exposed it dissolves.

91
00:05:45,507 --> 00:05:47,592
Where it was not, it stays.

92
00:05:47,592 --> 00:05:51,144
It is a positive resist,
so the light removes.

93
00:05:51,144 --> 00:05:57,013
The mask was dark over the gates, so
the stripes that survive are the gates.

94
00:05:57,013 --> 00:05:59,098
The cross-section shows it.

95
00:05:59,098 --> 00:06:04,735
The orange is left on the gate only,
and the field is down to bare oxide.

96
00:06:04,735 --> 00:06:06,974
The pattern finally shows up.

97
00:06:06,974 --> 00:06:11,453
From here the resist is the
mask that the next etch obeys.

98
00:06:13,487 --> 00:06:15,154
This is the second checkpoint.

99
00:06:15,154 --> 00:06:16,487
Development is finished,

100
00:06:16,487 --> 00:06:20,321
and the wafer goes under the microscope
at a hundred and fifty times.

101
00:06:20,321 --> 00:06:24,766
The pattern is in the right position, and
nothing is missing, doubled or broken.

102
00:06:24,766 --> 00:06:26,267
We can go to the next step.

103
00:06:28,307 --> 00:06:33,733
The resist cannot carry this
pattern through the whole gate etch.

104
00:06:33,733 --> 00:06:39,827
That etch is chlorine with oxygen in
it, and oxygen is what ashes resist.

105
00:06:39,827 --> 00:06:43,668
So we transfer the pattern
to the oxide first.

106
00:06:43,668 --> 00:06:48,009
That is this step: CHF₃ and
CF₄, fluorine chemistry,

107
00:06:48,009 --> 00:06:51,599
opening the oxide where the resist is open.

108
00:06:51,599 --> 00:06:58,361
It is timed, not endpointed, and it finishes
a little way into the silicon below.

109
00:06:58,361 --> 00:07:02,619
Fluorine etches sideways
as fast as it etches down,

110
00:07:02,619 --> 00:07:07,127
so the window comes out wider
than the resist opening.

111
00:07:09,187 --> 00:07:11,904
We use oxygen plasma to remove resist.

112
00:07:11,904 --> 00:07:14,479
The masking job passes to the oxide.

113
00:07:14,479 --> 00:07:19,341
From here the pattern is carried by
thirty-seven nanometres of SiO₂.

114
00:07:19,341 --> 00:07:24,848
Then we check under the microscope,
making sure the pattern is still correct.

115
00:07:24,848 --> 00:07:26,207
Then the main etch.

116
00:07:28,253 --> 00:07:31,860
The wafer goes in clean
and the mask is open.

117
00:07:31,860 --> 00:07:35,466
What is still in the
way is the native oxide:

118
00:07:35,466 --> 00:07:41,076
a nanometre or two of SiO₂ that grows
back whenever silicon meets air.

119
00:07:41,076 --> 00:07:43,800
Chlorine radicals cannot touch it.

120
00:07:43,800 --> 00:07:47,567
Only the ions can, and
they come straight down.

121
00:07:47,567 --> 00:07:51,253
That is the whole job of
these twelve seconds.

122
00:07:53,340 --> 00:07:56,818
The twelve seconds only opened the surface.

123
00:07:56,818 --> 00:08:00,376
An etch with no direction cannot make gates.

124
00:08:00,376 --> 00:08:04,663
So oxygen goes in: twenty
seconds of Cl₂, HBr and O₂.

125
00:08:04,663 --> 00:08:11,456
Oxygen arrives from every direction and
coats every exposed face with a thin Si–O–Cl

126
00:08:11,456 --> 00:08:12,556
layer.

127
00:08:12,556 --> 00:08:19,512
The ions come from above only, so they clear
that layer on the floor and nowhere else.

128
00:08:19,512 --> 00:08:23,637
The floor goes down; the
walls never move sideways.

129
00:08:23,637 --> 00:08:30,026
And when the floor reaches the gate oxide,
there is no silicon left to consume,

130
00:08:30,026 --> 00:08:35,526
so the chlorine radicals stop being
used up and their signal climbs.

131
00:08:35,526 --> 00:08:37,224
That is the endpoint.

132
00:08:37,224 --> 00:08:39,974
Etching keeps going a few seconds.

133
00:08:39,974 --> 00:08:44,099
The oxide etches twenty-five
to forty times slower,

134
00:08:44,099 --> 00:08:48,860
so an over-etch clears the last
patches without cutting into it.

135
00:08:50,687 --> 00:08:57,328
The monitor wafer goes throught the same
main etching recipe and comes back into the

136
00:08:57,328 --> 00:08:58,428
ellipsometry.

137
00:08:58,428 --> 00:09:01,354
Oxide stickness was 37.02 nanometres.

138
00:09:01,354 --> 00:09:02,619
It is now 33.06.

139
00:09:02,619 --> 00:09:06,810
3.96nm gone, and 89 per cent
of the mask still there.

140
00:09:06,810 --> 00:09:12,819
This implies that the hard mask only
loss 3.96nm stickness, far from broken.

141
00:09:14,807 --> 00:09:16,254
This is the last look.

142
00:09:16,254 --> 00:09:20,398
The gates are continuous: no
break, no bridge, no missing line.

143
00:09:20,398 --> 00:09:23,555
The widths marked on the
picture match the mask.

144
00:09:23,555 --> 00:09:27,370
And the cap is still on top,
which is how it should leave.

145
00:09:27,370 --> 00:09:33,026
The one thing optics cannot judge is the
sidewall, and that needs a SEM cross-section.

146
00:09:33,026 --> 00:09:36,447
So the wafer passes, and it
goes on to the next lab.

